  then a sectioned design, then a written spec for you to review. No code until you approve. Nothing has changed on
  disk.

  [1mFirst question — the one that determines everything else.[0m "Persist" can mean two different systems, and picking wrong
  means rebuilding:

  - [1mA durable identity.[0m A [38;5;153muserId[39m is established at login and survives page reloads, so any form in the app can read "who
    is the current user." Storage is client-side ([38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m/[38;5;153msessionStorage[39m), the module is a session accessor, and
    "other forms will need it" means they [3mread[0m it. Cheap, no backend work. Rules out: it's not trustworthy for anything
    security-sensitive — client-side identity is user-editable.
  - [1mAn audit trail of login events.[0m Each login is recorded as an event (who, when, outcome) and persisted somewhere
    durable — server-side. The module is a reporting/telemetry client. This is what you'd need if the point is answering
    "who logged in, and when" after the fact. Costs: needs a real backend endpoint; [38;5;153mAPI_ENDPOINT[39m is a stub today. Not
    cheap to retrofit later.
  - [1mBoth[0m, which is common: identity in the session for the app to read, plus events shipped to the server. Most work,
    but the two halves are separable, so you can stage them.

  [1mMy recommendation:[0m tell me which of these you're after before I propose approaches. "Other forms will need it later"
  leans toward the identity reading, but "persist" and "track who logged in" lean toward the audit trail — and those
  build almost nothing in common.
